\alias{PipeOpRandomEffectLong}
\title{Random-Effects Feature Extraction from a Longitudinal Table}
\description{
Fits a linear mixed model (random intercept and random slope over time) for each selected
longitudinal feature and appends the subject-specific random effects as new features to a survival
task (\code{TaskSurv}).

The PipeOp expects a \emph{long} table (class \code{"Long"}, typically a \code{data.table}) on a second input
channel and returns the augmented task on its output channel.
}
\details{
This PipeOp expects the input task to have exactly one group column (subject identifier) set via
\code{task$col_roles$group}. The longitudinal table must contain the subject id column \code{long_id_col}, the
time column \code{long_time_col}, and the selected longitudinal features.

During training, for each feature \eqn{y} in \code{feature_cols}, a linear mixed model is fit using
\pkg{lme4}:
\deqn{y \sim t + (1 + t \mid id)}
where \eqn{t} is the longitudinal time variable (\code{long_time_col}) and \eqn{id} is the longitudinal
subject identifier (\code{long_id_col}).

The per-subject random intercept and random slope are extracted and joined onto the task's data by
subject id, creating two new features per longitudinal feature:
\itemize{
\item \code{"<feature>_random_intercept"}
\item \code{"<feature>_random_slope"}
}

At prediction time, the PipeOp computes BLUP-style random effect estimates for each subject from
the stored training-time model parameters (fixed effects, random-effects covariance, residual
variance), using only the new longitudinal data provided at predict-time.
}
\section{Parameters}{

\describe{
\item{long_id_col (\code{character(1)})}{Column name in \code{long} holding the subject identifier (default: \code{"id"}).}
\item{long_time_col (\code{character(1)})}{Column name in \code{long} holding the measurement time (default: \code{"fuptime"}).}
\item{feature_cols (\code{character})}{Names of longitudinal feature columns in \code{long} to model. If \code{NULL},
numeric columns are auto-detected (excluding \code{long_id_col} and \code{long_time_col}).}
}
}

\section{Input and Output Channels}{

\describe{
\item{Input}{\code{"task"}: a \code{\link[mlr3:Task]{mlr3::Task}}, typically a \code{\link{TaskSurv}}.\cr
\code{"long"}: a longitudinal table of class \code{"Long"} containing \code{long_id_col}, \code{long_time_col}, and the
columns in \code{feature_cols}.}
\item{Output}{\code{"output"}: the input task augmented with random-effect features (a \code{\link{TaskSurv}}).}
}
}

\section{State}{

Stores the following values during training for reuse at predict-time:
\itemize{
\item \code{params}: per-feature model parameters (\code{beta}, \code{D}, \code{sigma2}),
\item resolved \code{feature_cols},
\item task group (id) column name,
\item \code{long_id_col}, \code{long_time_col}.
}
